        "link": "https://www.aljazeera.com/sports/liveblog/2026/4/15/live-bayern-munich-vs-real-madrid-champions-league-quarterfinal?traffic_source=rss",
        "orario": "18:00",
        "timestamp": 1776268800,
        "contenuto_esteso": "6 Updates Auto-updates live-orange 10m ago ( 16:50 GMT ) Bayern need to ‘push through the pain barrier’ Bayern Munich coach Vincent Kompany said his side need to be ready to “push through the pain barrier” to make it past Real Madrid and qualify for the Champions League semifinals. Kompany told reporters on Tuesday that Bayern’s desire against the 15-time European champions would be key. “It depends on many things. In games like this, the individual quality of the players is always decisive. But there’s also the collective intensity and a readiness to push through the pain barrier,” Kompany said. “Every detail matters in games like this, but for us what’s most important is that we’re mentally and physically well prepared. “We’re also looking forward to the match and that’s something you need, positive emotions.” live-orange 20m ago ( 16:40 GMT ) Real Madrid’s team news Thibaut Courtois and Rodrygo remain Real’s two biggest names on the sidelines through injury, but Los Blancos will also be without the services of Aurelien Tchouameni, who picked up his third yellow card of the competition in the first leg. The most pressing concern for Real is just how big a problem the facial injury sustained by Mbappe in training is. Lunin (goalkeeper); Alexander-Arnold, Rudiger, Huijsen, Carreras; Valverde, Camavinga, Bellingham; Guler; Mbappe, Vinicius Jr live-orange 30m ago ( 16:30 GMT ) Bayern Munich’s team news Winger Lennart Karl and goalkeeper Sven Ulreich are both out with injuries, but Harry Kane is expected to return after sitting out Saturday’s league match. The England striker reported “a few reactions” after the first leg in Madrid, but they are not expected to keep him out of the return match. Serge Gnabry has a minor knee problem, but he, too, is expected to be available. Neuer (goalkeeper); Laimer, Upamecano, Tah, Stanisic; Kimmich, Pavlovic; Olise, Gnabry, Diaz; Kane Advertisement live-orange 40m ago ( 16:20 GMT ) Atletico edge Barcelona on aggregate to reach Champions League semifinal In Tuesday’s other quarterfinal, Atletico Madrid sent 10-man Barcelona crashing out of the Champions League and reached the final four with a 3-2 aggregate victory , despite a 2-1 second leg defeat. Lamine Yamal and Ferran Torres fired visitors Barca ahead inside 24 minutes, but Ademola Lookman’s strike gave Atletico the edge in the gripping all-Spanish tie once again after their 2-0 win in the first leg. Diego Simeone’s side returned to the semifinals for the first time since 2017 by holding on against the La Liga champions in a compelling and bloody battle. Barcelona ended the game with 10 men as Eric Garcia was sent off for bringing down Alexander Sorloth as he ran in on goal, hampering their chance of finding a third goal to force extra time. Atletico, who have never won the competition and lost the 2014 and 2016 finals with Simeone at the helm, will face Arsenal or Sporting Lisbon in the semifinals. Read the full match report here . live-orange 50m ago ( 16:10 GMT ) PSG await in the semifinals Paris ‌St Germain showed why they are the reigning champions of Europe, coach ⁠Luis Enrique said, after ⁠his side completed a commanding 4-0 aggregate victory over Liverpool on Tuesday to reach the Champions League semifinals. PSG were pushed for spells at Anfield, particularly ⁠in the second half, but Ousmane Dembele struck twice after the break as Enrique’s men won 2-0 to again demonstrate the belief and resilience that are hallmarks of ⁠their European campaign PSG will face either Real Madrid or Bayern Munich, who meet on Wednesday with the Germans leading 2-1, in the ‌semifinals. Asked if he had a preference, Luis Enrique said: “If I say one team, we’ll end up playing the other team. “I’ll have a glass in my hand, I’ll be watching the ‌game ‌between Real Madrid and Bayern Munich, but it doesn’t matter, we deserve to be there and we’re going to make the most of it with our supporters.” live-orange 1h ago ( 16:00 GMT ) Welcome to our live coverage Hello, welcome and thanks for joining our live coverage as Bayern Munich host Real Madrid for the second leg of their blockbuster Champions League quarterfinal. Real Madrid’s season has seemed to teeter on the brink for most of the campaign, but with the La Liga title all but out of their reach, Los Blancos face their most daunting task yet as they seek to overturn Bayern Munich’s 2-1 aggregate lead in Germany. Having replaced Carlo Ancelotti, the most successful club manager in history, with Xabi Alonso last season, the Madrid-based club then changed leadership midseason after a string of poor results. Now, Alonso’s replacement, Alvaro Arbeloa, must try to topple the runaway German champions in their own stadium. I’m Patrick Keddie, and I’ll take you through our live build-up to the match along with all the photo coverage of the day. Adam Hancock will bring you the team sheets as they drop, as well as comprehensive text commentary of the game."
